These known facts are connected with the following conjecture of Freiman (personal communication), extending the \((3k-4)\)-theorem above.

**Conjecture 1**  *Let \(A\) be a finite subset of a torsion-free group with \(|A| \ge 4\). If*

\[
|A^2| \le 3|A| - 4
\]

*then \(A\) is covered by a progression of length at most \(2|A| - 3\).*

By using the so-called isoperimetric method, see Hamidoune [13], [14] or [15], we obtain the following results:

**Theorem 2**  *For any integer \(k \ge 1\) there exists a \(c(k)\) such that the following holds. If \(G\) is a torsion-free group, \(A \subseteq G\) is not contained in a left coset of any cyclic subgroup, \(B \subseteq G\) has more than \(c(k)\) elements, then*

\[
|AB| > |A| + |B| + k.
\]

**Remark**  Our current methods yield \(c(k) \le 32(k+3)^6\).

Note that, in Theorem 2, the assumption on \(A\) not being contained in a left coset of a cyclic group is crucial. For example, if \(A\) is a progression of length at most \(k+2\) with ratio \(r \ne 1\), and \(B\) is the union of two \(r\)-progressions of arbitrary length, then \(|AB| \le |A| + |B| + k\).

The value of the lower bound \(c(k)\) can be improved for unique product groups. Recall that a group \(G\) has the unique product property if, for every pair of finite sets \(A, B \subseteq G\), there is an element \(g \in AB\) which can be uniquely expressed as a product of an element of \(A\) and an element of \(B\). In this case, \(G\) is torsion-free. We note that every right linearly orderable group has the unique product property, and any residually finite word hyperbolic group has a finite index unique product subgroup, according to T. Delzant [6]. On the other hand, it was first shown by Rips and Segev [20] that not all torsion-free groups have the unique product property, and S.D. Promislow [19] even provided an explicit construction for such an example. In addition, unique product groups are discussed in A. Strojnowski [24], S.M. Hair [12], and W. Carter [5]. For unique product groups, the bound

